WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:04.760
Back in June at AI DevCon in London, we got four completely different answers

2
00:00:04.760 --> 00:00:09.640
to what sounds like a simple question where should the agent actually run?

3
00:00:09.640 --> 00:00:14.880
Let's first hear from Oleg Šelajev at Docker, who built a sandbox on containers,

4
00:00:14.920 --> 00:00:18.960
was told by enterprise security teams that containers are not a boundary

5
00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:22.880
they trust and went and rebuilt it on micro VMs.

6
00:00:23.560 --> 00:00:26.560
All right. Hi,

7
00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:27.560
my name is Oleg.

8
00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:29.360
I work at Docker.

9
00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:32.440
I'm a member of the DevRel team, doing all things

10
00:00:32.440 --> 00:00:35.560
AI at Docker, we thought about this,

11
00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:41.320
and when we were building sandboxes, we built one version with containers.

12
00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:44.960
We went out, we talked to a bunch of security teams

13
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:48.280
at enterprises, and all security teams said that no, containers

14
00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:52.560
are not the isolation boundary that we can trust.

15
00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:55.600
So being great engineers, what did we do?

16
00:00:55.600 --> 00:00:59.080
We rewrote that using micro VMs.

17
00:00:59.080 --> 00:01:02.760
So containers for micro VMs might not sound like

18
00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:05.800
such a big difference, but if you're sharing a kernel,

19
00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:08.920
if you have a number of security vulnerabilities for exploiting

20
00:01:08.920 --> 00:01:12.600
from outside of the container, that is not something that,

21
00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:18.240
well, a security team can sign off because it's going to be,

22
00:01:18.280 --> 00:01:21.520
well, their jobs and their reputation when the leaks will happen.

23
00:01:22.120 --> 00:01:23.280
And yeah, I'll bite.

24
00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:27.360
Container escaping exploits are not frequent.

25
00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:31.840
There are like a dozen over the last maybe six or seven or eight years.

26
00:01:31.880 --> 00:01:37.640
But as we discussed, you only need to be breached once and then it's.

27
00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:38.680
And then it's bad.

28
00:01:38.680 --> 00:01:43.960
So you want the hardware sort of virtual machine isolated primitive.

29
00:01:43.960 --> 00:01:48.400
And this is what Docker has built for the sandboxes, the SBX

30
00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:52.960
thingy that runs in micro on your machine.

31
00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:56.600
And then your agent is put there within the container.

32
00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:00.920
In addition to that you can this micro can run other containers.

33
00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:04.400
So if you are doing using agents for software development,

34
00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:08.120
the agent within this micro can do exactly what you would do.

35
00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:09.800
It can run tests.

36
00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:13.880
Your tests might spin up some containers, maybe with Testcontainers libraries,

37
00:02:13.880 --> 00:02:14.880
maybe

38
00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:18.720
just a normal Docker Compose approach, but they can create the full environment,

39
00:02:18.760 --> 00:02:21.720
run your applications, and that gives you much more control

40
00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:24.800
over how your agent can develop software, because it can do

41
00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:28.320
whatever you would do on your machine, just fully isolated.

42
00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:32.240
So and within that sandbox, within that,

43
00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:35.840
there is no access to the host file system.

44
00:02:35.880 --> 00:02:38.880
You choose what you share with the thing.

45
00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:40.600
You have the networking proxy.

46
00:02:40.600 --> 00:02:43.280
So all the requests out of the sandbox go through that.

47
00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:48.280
So you have control saying, please forbid all access to Pastebin.

48
00:02:48.280 --> 00:02:52.680
So if the agent wants to steal the keys, it cannot do that this way.

49
00:02:52.720 --> 00:02:56.360
And there is also a secrets injection mechanism.

50
00:02:56.360 --> 00:02:59.720
So the agent inside doesn't have access to your private data

51
00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:03.080
while still be able to do things on your behalf.

52
00:03:03.240 --> 00:03:07.200
We also heard from Luke Marsden, CEO of Helix ML,

53
00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:10.640
who takes the opposite view — from your laptop, entirely.

54
00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:14.280
Put the agents on the organization's infrastructure

55
00:03:14.280 --> 00:03:17.800
so the work can be handed between people in different time zones.

56
00:03:17.800 --> 00:03:21.280
His line is that you should give each agent their own computer.

57
00:03:21.640 --> 00:03:24.320
The first big

58
00:03:24.320 --> 00:03:27.520
decision, in my opinion, is: do you want to carry on

59
00:03:27.520 --> 00:03:32.480
letting every developer run whatever agent they like, or maybe

60
00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:36.040
the same agent that everyone uses in the company,

61
00:03:36.040 --> 00:03:38.600
but on their own specific development environment?

62
00:03:38.600 --> 00:03:40.360
That's kind of like a snowflake.

63
00:03:40.360 --> 00:03:46.240
Or do you want to create a pool of

64
00:03:46.240 --> 00:03:50.560
agents that run on your organization's infrastructure

65
00:03:50.600 --> 00:03:53.600
that many humans can interact with?

66
00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:58.200
And my strong argument is that.

67
00:03:59.560 --> 00:04:01.400
Going for the centralized approach

68
00:04:01.400 --> 00:04:03.720
and I say centralized, it doesn't mean you have to

69
00:04:03.720 --> 00:04:07.440
hand over all your data to OpenAI or run on their infrastructure or anything.

70
00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:09.360
You can run it on your own infrastructure.

71
00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:12.800
Still on Kubernetes or something, but there are

72
00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:16.960
some significant benefits from having a centralized approach.

73
00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:23.200
For example, you can have a globally distributed team

74
00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:26.440
all around the world, and you can say

75
00:04:26.440 --> 00:04:31.440
that when the sun sets in Tokyo and rises in London,

76
00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:35.880
whoever a different human can carry on doing the work

77
00:04:35.920 --> 00:04:40.240
that a certain agent was working on, especially if that agent truly

78
00:04:40.240 --> 00:04:43.240
has its own computer for that specific task.

79
00:04:43.880 --> 00:04:46.240
And so there's a really nice

80
00:04:46.240 --> 00:04:49.400
article written by by someone.

81
00:04:49.400 --> 00:04:53.400
We've been working with a company called Dev Icon,

82
00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:57.480
and what they said was, to be honest, agentic coding is not new.

83
00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:00.560
Going from doing it the easier way, which is the way on the left

84
00:05:00.560 --> 00:05:03.600
which is unsafe and per engineer to something global per company

85
00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:07.280
and safe is what they found interesting in this approach.

86
00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:08.760
So that's kind of my argument.

87
00:05:08.760 --> 00:05:10.640
My opinion number one is

88
00:05:11.640 --> 00:05:12.440
you should

89
00:05:12.440 --> 00:05:15.720
give each agent their own computer, not each human.

90
00:05:15.720 --> 00:05:17.080
It's almost like you wouldn't hire

91
00:05:17.080 --> 00:05:20.320
a team of software developers and ask them all to share one computer.

92
00:05:20.360 --> 00:05:23.360
You should give each agent their own computer.

93
00:05:23.720 --> 00:05:27.840
Lars Trieloff is a principal scientist at Adobe, and he built an agent

94
00:05:27.840 --> 00:05:31.480
that runs inside the browser and drives the browser it's running in.

95
00:05:31.720 --> 00:05:36.160
There's also a reveal at the end of his talk that I'll let him deliver himself.

96
00:05:36.880 --> 00:05:38.480
How do we get started with this?

97
00:05:38.480 --> 00:05:41.840
And the.

98
00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:46.800
The idea that I came up with was an agent that is sitting in the browser.

99
00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:51.280
It's actually running in the browser, which means it's not a cloud runtime

100
00:05:51.280 --> 00:05:56.240
where the agent loop runs and you're just displaying in the browser,

101
00:05:56.240 --> 00:05:59.560
but it's actually the actual agent loop is in the browser.

102
00:05:59.560 --> 00:06:02.560
To close the browser tab, the agent loop starts,

103
00:06:02.920 --> 00:06:08.080
and at the same time this agent should be able to control itself.

104
00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:10.800
It should control it should

105
00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:13.400
it should control the browser that it's running inside.

106
00:06:13.400 --> 00:06:17.800
And this is how I came up with this notion of the self-licking ice cream cone.

107
00:06:18.080 --> 00:06:23.600
And that's why you find it at SLICC,

108
00:06:24.560 --> 00:06:25.560
sliccy.com

109
00:06:25.560 --> 00:06:29.800
And while building it,

110
00:06:29.960 --> 00:06:33.360
there are a bunch of things that I was absolutely sure.

111
00:06:33.440 --> 00:06:35.360
Well it's a browser right.

112
00:06:35.360 --> 00:06:36.960
It can display HTML pages.

113
00:06:36.960 --> 00:06:39.800
But there are a lot of things that it suddenly cannot do right.

114
00:06:39.800 --> 00:06:42.280
So cannot transcode videos.

115
00:06:42.280 --> 00:06:45.280
It suddenly cannot access my local file system.

116
00:06:45.800 --> 00:06:48.760
But I'm fine with these constraints.

117
00:06:48.760 --> 00:06:53.040
But in reality, I found during this exploration

118
00:06:53.080 --> 00:06:54.200
that there are so many things

119
00:06:54.200 --> 00:06:59.080
that my browser can do that I didn't even dare to think about.

120
00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:04.040
And that was one of the things that made this exploration extremely fun.

121
00:07:04.080 --> 00:07:08.560
And now you're wondering what this what this slide means.

